\section{Pair-to-Batch Aggregation Simulation}
\label{app:aggregation-sim}

The following simulation provides an illustrative explanatory model for how pair-level effects aggregate to batch-level variance. It is not the evidence base for our claims; the observed batch-level $R^2 = 0.218$ (\S\ref{sec:practical_magnitude}) is a direct empirical measurement independent of this simulation.

\paragraph{Setup.} We examine whether the pair-level $R^2 \approx 7\%$ (computed over non-zero confusion pairs) can aggregate to the observed batch-level $R^2 \approx 22\%$ via a Monte Carlo simulation. From the FewRel 16-relation experiment, we estimate the inter-pair SCS correlation for pairs sharing a common type ($\rho_\text{SCS} = 0.35$). For batches of $k=8$ types ($\binom{8}{2}=28$ pairs), we simulate 10{,}000 iterations: (1) generate 28 correlated pair-level effects with individual $R^2 = 0.074$; (2) average to obtain batch-level signals; (3) compute batch-level $R^2$.

\paragraph{Results.} Simulated batch $R^2 = 0.219$ [95\% CI: 0.092--0.361], matching the observed value of 0.218. The effective amplification factor is $\sim$3$\times$, explained by signal correlation within batches preserving variance while independent noise averages out (effective degrees of freedom $\approx 3.4$, not 28).

\paragraph{Unconditional baseline.}
When the simulation uses the unconditional pairwise $r^2 = 0.013$ (computed over all type pairs including zero-confusion pairs; Table~\ref{tab:pairwise}), the simulated batch-level $R^2$ drops to $0.044$ [95\% CI: 0.001--0.151], well below the observed $R^2 = 0.218$. The analytical formula confirms: $\text{SNR} \times \text{EF} / (\text{SNR} \times \text{EF} + 1) = 0.042$ with the same effective factor $\text{EF} = 3.35$. This ${\sim}5\times$ gap reflects the dilution from zero-confusion pairs: approximately 89\% of type pairs never exhibit mutual confusion, contributing only noise to the unconditional correlation. The conditional model ($R^2 = 0.074 \to 0.219$) matches the observed value because it isolates the minority of semantically proximate pairs that actually compete during type resolution; BCS predicts batch accuracy through the density of \emph{active} confusable pairs, not uniform contributions from all pairs.

\section{BCS Independence from Type Composition}
\label{app:bcs-composition}

FewRel's balanced design (700 instances per type) makes type frequency invariant across batches, ruling out frequency-driven confounding. To test whether BCS captures information beyond type composition effects, we decompose the explained variance in a joint model (accuracy $\sim$ BCS $+$ mean type difficulty), where type difficulty is the per-type solo-baseline accuracy averaged across batch members. On 100 random 8-type batches from the 16-relation pool, the joint model explains $R^2 = 0.704$. Variance decomposition yields: BCS unique $R^2 = 0.259$ (36.8\% of explained variance), composition unique $R^2 = 0.298$ (42.3\%), and shared $R^2 = 0.147$ (20.9\%). The near-orthogonality of predictors (VIF${}\,=\,1.06$, $r(\text{BCS}, \text{difficulty}) = -0.21$) confirms that BCS captures confusability-specific signal rather than proxying for type difficulty.

\section{Split-Origin Covariate Analysis}
\label{app:split-origin}

FewRel combines 64 train-split and 16 val-split relation types into a single 80-type pool.  Train-origin types show numerically lower accuracy ($-8.8$\,pp, $p = 0.06$; see Limitations), raising the question of whether differential pre-training exposure confounds the BCS--accuracy relationship.  We test this by adding a per-batch \emph{train-origin ratio} (fraction of relation types drawn from the train split) as a covariate in the BCS--accuracy regression.

\paragraph{Method.}  Across 213 multi-type batches ($k \geq 2$) from the batch-composition experiment (\S\ref{sec:sdgd}) and the factorial design (\S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}), we compute $\texttt{train\_origin\_ratio} = n_{\text{train}} / k$ for each batch.  The ratio ranges from 0.40 to 1.00 (mean 0.80, reflecting the 64/80 base rate).  We fit:
\[
\text{accuracy} = \beta_0 + \beta_{\text{BCS}} \cdot \text{BCS} + \beta_{\text{origin}} \cdot \text{train\_origin\_ratio} + \varepsilon
\]

\paragraph{Results.}  The BCS partial effect is unchanged after controlling for split-origin: $\beta_{\text{BCS}} = -0.416$, $p < 10^{-16}$ (vs.\ $\beta = -0.412$, $p < 10^{-16}$ without the covariate).  Train-origin ratio is not independently significant ($\beta_{\text{origin}} = -0.057$, $p = 0.245$), and $\Delta R^2 = 0.005$.  The BCS--accuracy relationship is therefore not confounded by differential pre-training exposure at the batch level.

Pairwise SCS distributions do not differ significantly between train-origin (64 types, mean\,=\,0.880) and val-origin (16 types, mean\,=\,0.874) subsets (Mann-Whitney $p = 0.754$, Cohen's $d = 0.081$), confirming that the train/val split does not introduce systematic confusability bias.

\section{Cross-Seed Variance Decomposition}
\label{app:variance-decomposition}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{rrrr}
\toprule
\textbf{Count} & \textbf{Batch SD} & \textbf{Seed SD} & \textbf{Ratio} \\
\midrule
4 & 0.0889 & 0.0122 & 53.5 \\
8 & 0.0820 & 0.0098 & 70.0 \\
16 & 0.0626 & 0.0270 & 5.4 \\
32 & 0.0462 & 0.0091 & 26.0 \\
40 & 0.0421 & 0.0019 & 474.4 \\
64 & 0.0245 & 0.0022 & 119.8 \\
80 & 0.0001 & 0.0088 & $<$0.1 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Variance decomposition on FewRel (3 seeds $\times$ 25 batches per count level). Batch SD: root-mean intra-seed variance averaged over seeds. Seed SD: standard deviation of per-seed means. Ratio = batch variance / seed variance. At count 80, only one batch composition exists, so batch variance vanishes by construction.}
\label{tab:variance_decomposition}
\end{table}

Intra-batch variance dominates inter-seed variance by 5--474$\times$ across count levels 4--64 (mean 125$\times$), confirming that random batch composition is the primary source of performance variability. Under greedy decoding (temperature${}\,=\,0$), model inference is nearly deterministic; the inter-seed variance reflects differences in which random batch compositions each seed generates, not inference stochasticity. At count 80, the full schema admits only one composition, leaving residual model-level noise as the sole variance source (seed SD = 0.88\,pp, likely attributable to FP8 quantization non-determinism).

\section{Cross-Domain Connections}
\label{app:cross-domain}

The schema-induced degradation documented here is not unique to information extraction. Schema-Guided Dialogue systems (SGD/SGD-X; \citealt{rastogi2020towards}) face an analogous challenge: as the number of intent and slot types grows, dialogue state tracking accuracy declines. \citet{li2023evaluating} observed count-dependent degradation in ChatGPT-based IE, attributing it to prompt overload. Our two-dimensional decomposition offers a more refined explanation: schema size per se is not the sole predictor; rather, larger schemas increase the probability of including high-SCS pairs, which contribute disproportionately to confusion. This distinction has practical implications: a schema of 80 types with low mutual confusability may outperform a 40-type schema with clustered semantics, and suggests that confusability-aware design principles may generalize to any task where an LLM must discriminate among a prompt-specified label inventory.

\section{Cross-Model Validation with LLaMA-3.1-70B-Instruct (FP8)}
\label{sec:appendix_cross_model_70b}

To test whether the two-dimensional degradation pattern generalizes across model families, we replicate the count-curve and BCS-controlled experiments on Meta-LLaMA-3.1-70B-Instruct (FP8 quantization). All batch compositions, type pools, and test samples are identical to those used for Qwen3-32B (\S\ref{sec:count_curves}, \S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lrr}
\toprule
\textbf{Count} & \textbf{LLaMA-70B} & \textbf{Qwen3-32B} \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{RE (FewRel, Classification Accuracy)}} \\
8  & 0.757 & 0.805 \\
16 & 0.685 & 0.719 \\
32 & 0.601 & 0.652 \\
64 & 0.504 & 0.539 \\
80 & 0.450 & 0.504 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{NER (Few-NERD, Text+Type Match Recall)}} \\
2  & 0.323 & 0.515 \\
6  & 0.300 & 0.399 \\
12 & 0.278 & 0.318 \\
22 & 0.252 & 0.279 \\
44 & 0.297 & 0.309 \\
66 & 0.273 & 0.407 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Cross-model count curves for LLaMA-3.1-70B-Instruct (FP8) and Qwen3-32B. RE decline (count 8$\to$80): $-$30.7\,pp (70B) vs.\ $-$30.1\,pp (32B). NER decline (count 2$\to$22): $-$7.1\,pp (70B) vs.\ $-$23.6\,pp (32B); both models exhibit a U-shaped rebound at higher counts. 25 batches per count level, same samples and batch compositions across models (seed 42). All metrics are micro-averaged across batches.}
\label{tab:cross_model_70b}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:cross_model_70b} shows that RE degradation slopes are near-identical: $-$30.7\,pp (70B) versus $-$30.1\,pp (32B) from count 8 to count 80, with the 70B model approximately 4\,pp lower in absolute accuracy at each count level. This quantitative match provides strong evidence that Dimension~1 (count-driven decline) is directionally consistent across architectures for RE.

On NER, the count-driven decline direction is consistent across models: both 70B and Qwen3 show a recall decline from count 2 to count 22 (70B: 0.323$\to$0.252, $-$7.1\,pp; Qwen3: 0.515$\to$0.279, $-$23.6\,pp). Both models also exhibit a U-shaped rebound at higher counts (70B: 0.252 at count 22 $\to$ 0.297 at count 44; Qwen3: 0.279 at count 22 $\to$ 0.407 at count 66), which cross-validates the U-shape phenomenon. The magnitude difference reflects a precision-recall tradeoff: 70B operates more conservatively (higher precision, lower recall), while Qwen3 over-predicts (higher recall, lower precision, especially at low counts).

For the confusability dimension (Dimension 2), at count 16 on FewRel, LLaMA-70B achieves low-BCS accuracy of 0.708 versus high-BCS accuracy of 0.599 ($\Delta = +10.9$\,pp, 95\% CI: [$+$3.0, $+$20.1]). The confidence interval excludes zero, confirming the confusability effect at the 70B scale.

In summary, FewRel RE provides a quantitative match across models (near-identical slopes for Dimension~1 and a significant Dimension~2 effect). NER provides directional consistency: the count-driven decline direction is preserved and the U-shaped rebound replicates, though magnitudes vary with each model's precision-recall operating point. Together, these results indicate that the degradation pattern is directionally consistent across architectures.

\section{Few-Shot Prompting and Confusability}
\label{sec:appendix_fewshot_bcs}

To test whether in-context demonstrations mitigate the BCS-driven confusability effect, we evaluate 0-shot, 1-shot, and 5-shot prompting on FewRel 80-relation RE at count 8 using the same extreme-BCS batch design as \S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}. Each condition uses 50 batches (25 low-BCS, mean $\approx 0.36$; 25 high-BCS, mean $\approx 0.80$), drawn from the pre-constructed batch pool of \S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}. Few-shot examples are sampled from the FewRel training split for each target type.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{\begin{tabular}{lrrrl}
\toprule
\textbf{Condition} & \textbf{Low-BCS} & \textbf{High-BCS} & \textbf{$\Delta$ (pp)} & \textbf{95\% CI} \\
\midrule
0-shot & 0.682 & 0.498 & +18.5 & {[}+14.3, +22.7{]} \\
1-shot & 0.754 & 0.554 & +20.0 & {[}+16.3, +23.5{]} \\
5-shot & 0.715 & 0.488 & +22.8 & {[}+18.9, +26.8{]} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{BCS gap under few-shot prompting (FewRel, count 8). Demonstrations improve absolute accuracy on low-BCS batches (+3.3--7.2\,pp over 0-shot) but provide inconsistent benefit on high-BCS batches (+5.6\,pp at 1-shot, $-$1.0\,pp at 5-shot). The confusability gap widens from +18.5\,pp (0-shot) to +22.8\,pp (5-shot). All metrics are micro-averaged across batches.}
\label{tab:fewshot_bcs}
\end{table}

Demonstrations improve absolute accuracy primarily on low-BCS (easy) batches, where type boundaries are already distinct and examples reinforce correct mappings. On high-BCS batches, where types are semantically confusable, demonstrations provide inconsistent benefit (+5.6\,pp at 1-shot, $-$1.0\,pp at 5-shot): the model still substitutes similar types at comparable rates. The net effect is that the confusability gap widens under few-shot prompting (+4.3\,pp from 0-shot to 5-shot, bootstrap $p = 0.014$), with all three confidence intervals excluding zero. This indicates that BCS-driven type confusion is a structural property of the schema composition rather than a deficit addressable through prompt engineering. The non-monotonic low-BCS pattern (0.754 at 1-shot vs.\ 0.715 at 5-shot) may reflect lost-in-middle effects from increased prompt length \cite{lost-in-middle-2024}.

The 0-shot gap (+18.5\,pp) is smaller than the extreme-batch gap reported in \S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled} (+25.5\,pp) because the two experiments use different test splits and batch sampling seeds; direct magnitude comparison across experiments is not appropriate.

\end{document}

