\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Boundary conditions.}
The decomposition applies to cross-domain schemas; same-domain schemas exhibit centrality confounding (\S\ref{sec:centrality_confound}). High-BCS accuracy converges to $\approx$51\% across models, suggesting a difficulty ceiling. RE's separation may partly reflect its closed-set format; the effect generalizes to document-level RE (DocRED slope $-$0.71). Prompt design modulates but does not eliminate the effect (v2 gap: $-$25.5\,pp; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_prompt_sensitivity}). Cross-scale evidence (32B vs.\ 8B) shows Dimension~1 replicates fully while Dimension~2 attenuates on smaller models, dropping to near-zero $R^2$ for NER on Llama-3.1-8B (Tables~\ref{tab:cross_model}--\ref{tab:cross_model_bcs}). Count-related degradation may partly reflect prompt length (per-type recall 0.586 vs.\ single-pass 0.368, consistent with lost-in-middle effects; \citealt{lost-in-middle-2024}; \citealt{levy-2024-same-task}).

\paragraph{Why does embedding similarity predict LLM confusion?}
SCS captures semantic proximity in LLMs' type-resolution space. The unconditional pair-level effect is modest ($r^2 = 1.3$--$1.7\%$, as ${\sim}89\%$ of pairs never produce mutual confusion); among active-confusion pairs, $R^2 \approx 7\%$ amplifies ${\sim}3\times$ at batch level (Appendix~\ref{app:aggregation-sim}), yielding $R^2$ of 22--53\%. For individual type pairs, SCS is therefore a weak predictor; other factors such as frequency, context diversity, and definition quality dominate. At the batch level, however, these pair-level effects aggregate to produce the observed $R^2$ of 22--53\% in RE, sufficient for practical schema-level screening. We hypothesize that the NER U-shape (\S\ref{sec:count_curves}) reflects a dual-task burden absent in RE: partial schemas require simultaneous span detection and type-membership filtering; at full-schema, filtering becomes vacuous and recall recovers, confirming task-specific pathways.

\paragraph{Task-dependent batch-level effects.}
While pairwise SCS predicts type confusion in both NER and RE, the batch-level BCS--performance relationship is RE-specific: BCS explains 22--53\% of cross-batch accuracy variance in RE but shows no significant correlation with NER F1 ($r \approx 0$, $n = 450$). We hypothesize this reflects the different error structures: RE errors are dominated by type substitution (IntraSub scales with BCS; \S\ref{sec:multi_channel}), so batch-level confusability directly predicts accuracy. NER errors are dominated by missed entities (43--65\% of errors), which correlate with count rather than confusability; pairwise SCS captures individual type confusion but this signal is diluted at the batch level by the overwhelming contribution of boundary detection failures. This task dependence has practical implications: BCS is a useful pre-hoc screening tool for RE schema design but not for NER, where count reduction is the primary lever.

\paragraph{Practical implications.}
The bounded count$\times$confusability interaction (0.47\,pp, TOST $p = 0.003$) is itself an actionable finding: practitioners who scale schema size need not additionally worry about confusability amplification of count-related degradation. For RE deployment with cross-domain schemas, BCS provides a lightweight pre-hoc screen: schemas that exceed the top-quartile BCS threshold (mean pairwise SCS $> 0.59$) can expect 3--5\,pp lower accuracy, which can inform decisions about few-shot augmentation or schema redesign. For NER, count is the dominant predictor, and the primary mitigation strategy is to reduce schema size per prompt. Even few-shot demonstrations do not mitigate the confusability gap: under 5-shot prompting, the BCS penalty persists and slightly widens (+4.3\,pp over 0-shot, bootstrap $p = 0.014$), suggesting confusability is a structural property of the schema rather than a prompt engineering problem (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_fewshot_bcs}). These results apply to the training-free setting; fine-tuned models may learn type boundaries that reduce confusability effects.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Schema-induced degradation in training-free LLM extraction operates along two dimensions (count-related recall decline, confusability-related type confusion) with bounded association in RE but coupling in NER. Dimension~1 replicates across scales; Dimension~2 attenuates on smaller models. BCS enables pre-hoc screening.

\section*{Responsible NLP Research Checklist}

For each question, the answer is \textbf{Yes}, \textbf{No}, or \textbf{N/A}. If Yes, the relevant section is indicated. If No, a justification is provided.

\subsection*{A \quad For Every Submission}

\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{A\arabic*.},leftmargin=*,itemsep=4pt]

\item \textbf{Did you describe the limitations of your work?}

Yes. The Limitations section (after \S\ref{sec:conclusion}) discusses single-model reliance, modest pairwise effect sizes, cross-pool slope consistency, factorial interaction tests with TOST equivalence, decomposition scope, and metric scope.

\item \textbf{Did you discuss any potential risks of your work?}

N/A. This is a purely analytical study of schema-induced degradation in existing LLM IE systems. It does not introduce new models, datasets, or deployment-facing artifacts. The findings characterize failure modes and are intended to inform safer schema design.

\item \textbf{Do the abstract and introduction summarize the paper's main claims?}

Yes. The abstract and \S\ref{sec:introduction} state the two-dimensional degradation pattern (count and confusability), the cross-model consistency check, and the negative SDGD result.

\item \textbf{Did you use AI assistants (e.g., ChatGPT, Copilot) when working on this paper?}

Yes. We used Claude (Anthropic) for writing assistance and experimental code development. All scientific claims, experimental designs, and analyses were conducted by the authors.

\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{B \quad Scientific Artifacts}

\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{B\arabic*.},leftmargin=*,itemsep=4pt]

\item \textbf{Did you cite the creators of artifacts you used?}

Yes. Few-NERD \cite{fewnerd-2021}, FewRel \cite{fewrel-2018}, DocRED \cite{docred-2019}, MAVEN \cite{maven-2020}, Qwen3-32B \cite{qwen3-2025}, Meta-Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct and Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct \cite{llama3-2024}, vLLM \cite{vllm-2023}, and all-MiniLM-L6-v2 \cite{minilm-2020} are cited in \S\ref{sec:datasets} and \S\ref{sec:model}.

\item \textbf{Did you discuss the license or terms for use and/or distribution of any artifacts?}

Yes. All four datasets are publicly available research benchmarks. Few-NERD is released under CC-BY-SA 4.0; FewRel, DocRED, and MAVEN are publicly released for research use. Models are used under their respective open-weight licenses.

\item \textbf{Did you discuss if your use of existing artifacts is consistent with their intended use?}

Yes. All datasets are used for their intended purpose (IE evaluation). No data is used outside the scope specified by the dataset creators.

\item \textbf{Did you discuss whether the data contains personally identifiable information or offensive content?}

N/A. We use established benchmarks that have been vetted by the research community. No new data collection or annotation was performed.

\item \textbf{Did you provide documentation of the artifacts, e.g., coverage of languages, coverage of domains, etc.?}

Yes. Table~\ref{tab:datasets} reports type counts, evaluation unit counts, domain characteristics, and BCS ranges for each dataset. \S\ref{sec:datasets} describes dataset provenance and sampling procedures.

\item \textbf{Did you report relevant statistics for your dataset(s)?}

Yes. \S\ref{sec:datasets} and Table~\ref{tab:datasets} report evaluation set sizes, type counts, and sampling methodology. Count levels and batch sizes are specified in \S\ref{sec:exp_design}.

\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{C \quad Computational Experiments}

\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{C\arabic*.},leftmargin=*,itemsep=4pt]

\item \textbf{Did you report the number of parameters in the models used, the total computational budget, and computing infrastructure?}

Yes. \S\ref{sec:model}: Qwen3-32B (32B parameters, FP8), Meta-Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct (70B parameters, FP8), and Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct (8B parameters). Inference was conducted on NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 GPUs (96\,GB) served via vLLM. Total compute was approximately 72 GPU-hours of inference (no training).

\item \textbf{Did you discuss the experimental setup, including hyperparameter search and best-found values?}

Yes. \S\ref{sec:exp_design} describes both experimental paradigms (count curves and BCS-controlled). \S\ref{sec:model} reports decoding parameters (greedy, temperature${}=0$, seed${}=42$) and prompt versions. No hyperparameter search was conducted as this is a zero-shot evaluation study.

\item \textbf{Did you report descriptive statistics about your results?}

Yes. All key metrics include 95\% bootstrap confidence intervals (10{,}000 resamples, percentile method) as stated in \S\ref{sec:model}. Spearman correlations are reported with $p$-values throughout \S\ref{sec:count_curves}--\ref{sec:centrality_confound}. Permutation tests are used for slope comparisons.

\item \textbf{Did you report implementation details, e.g., versions of packages?}

Yes. \S\ref{sec:model} specifies vLLM v0.20.2 for model serving. Embedding models are named with version identifiers (\S\ref{sec:scs}). Code and scripts will be released upon acceptance.

\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{D \quad Human Annotators or Crowdworkers}

\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{D\arabic*.},leftmargin=*,itemsep=4pt]

